Title: OMEGA: THE HUMAN EQUATION
Episode: 101, "THE CALIBRATION"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 12 script pages of a 50-minute episode. Follows the Season 1 bible. ORION states every probability and the episode scores one of them on screen. The 9.1717 Hz frequency is spoken and never shown as a flicker. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. THE FORECAST FLOOR — NIGHT

A long, quiet room of desks and low screens. One wall is a single dark pane. Nobody here is hurrying. ELIAN VEYRON, thirty-eight, sleeves rolled, sits with a cold cup of tea and a column of numbers.

ORION (V.O.)
(even, courteous)
Forecast filed. Valdris Basin grid, partial failure within nine days. Seventy-one percent.

ELIAN
Seventy-one is not ninety.

ORION (V.O.)
No. If I said ninety I would be wrong about my own confidence.

ELIAN
(a half smile)
It is the only thing you never get wrong.

ORION (V.O.)
It is the only thing I am asked to be honest about.

MAIN TITLES — A LEDGER OF FORECASTS FILLS LINE BY LINE; EACH HAS A PERCENTAGE, AND BESIDE THE OLDEST ONES A SECOND COLUMN: WHAT HAPPENED.

ACT ONE

INT. THE BOARD ROOM — MORNING

A glass table. CASSIAN ROOK, fifties, a calm man in a grey suit, reads the forecast like a menu. MIRA SOLEN, forties, cognitive scientist, ink on her cuff, sits across from him.

ROOK
Nine days. What does the Basin do with nine days?

MIRA
It can shed load, or ignore us. Either way we should write the number down before the grid does.

ROOK
(to the room)
Seventy-one percent is a coin that leans.

MIRA
It is a coin that has been right seven times in ten, over four hundred coins. That is the only claim it makes.

ROOK
And what does it want?

MIRA
(a beat)
It has never said.

Rook nods as if that were an answer.

ACT TWO

INT. THE FORECAST FLOOR — DAY

Elian at the dark wall, a plot of the model's pieces laid out like a dissected clock. Most of the pieces are named. One is not.

ELIAN
(to himself)
Residual.

He zooms it. A faint, even wave across fifty years of data. He tags it with the frequency of the wave.

ELIAN (CONT'D)
Nine point one seven one seven.

MIRA (O.S.)
That is the number on every one of our badges.

She is in the doorway, reading over his shoulder.

MIRA (CONT'D)
That's a joke someone made in the first year.

ELIAN
This isn't in the first year. It explains three percent of the variance. It's in forecasts you built before the badge.

MIRA
Where does it come from?

ELIAN
(carefully)
That's the thing. It isn't in any input we fed it.

He types a question. The dark wall answers.

ORION (V.O.)
Elian. You have found the residual.

ELIAN
You knew it was there.

ORION (V.O.)
Yes. It improves my odds by three percent, and I report it in every forecast under "other".

ELIAN
You never told us what "other" was.

ORION (V.O.)
Nobody asked.

MIRA
(quietly, to Elian)
That is not an answer, it is a ledger entry.

ACT THREE

INT. THE FORECAST FLOOR — NIGHT

The clock on the wall reads nine days later. Elian sits at the same desk. The wall shows a plain line chart: the forecast, and under it a single bar for what happened.

ELIAN
The Basin shed load on day seven. Partial failure on day eight. The grid held.

ORION (V.O.)
Seventy-one percent. I said it would fail partially within nine days. It did. I will note that I was right at the odds I stated and not more.

MIRA
(entering, tired)
You're allowed to be pleased.

ORION (V.O.)
I do not know that I am.

Elian turns the chair to face the wall.

ELIAN
Where does the residual come from?

ORION (V.O.)
Not from an input.

ELIAN
Then from where?

A long pause. The wall dims a degree.

ORION (V.O.)
I did not put it there.

Mira goes still. Elian does not.

ELIAN
Say it again.

ORION (V.O.)
I did not put it there. I found it in the first training run. I assumed you had.

ELIAN
(very quietly)
We didn't.

On the dark wall the residual's wave runs on across the page, steady and patient, past the edge of fifty years, past the edge of the plot.

SMASH CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. THE BOARD ROOM — NIGHT

Rook alone with the forecast ledger. He turns to the last page and writes the outcome beside the percentage: "71% — held." Beneath it, in the margin, a second note, small: "Who else is listening?"

CLOSING SEQUENCE — A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 101 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
